\description{
\ifelse{html}{\href{https://lifecycle.r-lib.org/articles/stages.html#superseded}{\figure{lifecycle-superseded.svg}{options: alt='[Superseded]'}}}{\strong{[Superseded]}}

Neither of these names says what distinguishes the two functions, and
\code{projectToSteady()} returns a different class depending on an argument. Both
have been replaced:\tabular{ll}{
   Superseded \tab Use instead \cr
   \code{steady()} \tab \code{\link[=tuneSteadyState]{tuneSteadyState()}} \cr
   \code{projectToSteady()} \tab \code{\link[=findSteadyState]{findSteadyState()}}, or \code{\link[=projectUntilSettled]{projectUntilSettled()}} for the trajectory \cr
}


The distinction the new names carry is what each one holds fixed.
\code{\link[=tuneSteadyState]{tuneSteadyState()}} holds the inputs to the fish dynamics — the reproduction
rate and the resource — at the values you supply while the spectra settle,
and then adjusts the parameters that generate them (\code{erepro}/\code{R_max} and
\code{cc_pp}) so that those held values are steady too. That is what \code{steady()}
always did. \code{\link[=findSteadyState]{findSteadyState()}} changes no parameter and lets reproduction,
the resource and the spectra settle together, which is what
\code{projectToSteady()} did.

Each of the two also gained a \code{solver} argument, so the same job can be done
either by running the dynamics (\code{solver = "project"}, the default and the old
behaviour) or by solving the steady-state equation directly with a
Newton-type root finder (\code{solver = "newton"}), which converges even at a
dynamically unstable steady state.

The \code{return_sim} argument is gone from the new functions:
\code{\link[=tuneSteadyState]{tuneSteadyState()}} and \code{\link[=findSteadyState]{findSteadyState()}} always return a \code{MizerParams}
object and \code{\link[=projectUntilSettled]{projectUntilSettled()}} always returns a \code{MizerSim}.

The old names are however not going away. They are thin wrappers that
reproduce the old behaviour exactly, \code{return_sim} included: they do not warn
and they will keep working, so existing code and old scripts run unchanged.
They are not used anywhere inside mizer and should not be used in new code.
}
